div.p:
In a place where philosophy or the love of wisdom is so universally professed and so carefully cultivated, where so many are obliged by their office to study and practise this particular sort of wisdom, and where more are designed and endeavouring to qualify themselves for the same holy function, it can’t but be highly proper to make that wisdom the subject of our consideration, which a great part of us are engaged by so peculiar
ties to recommend both by our lives and doctrines;
The majority of Wesley’s audience would have been ordained priests; cf. BCP, Ordering of Priests, opening and closing collects. and to explain and enforce this important truth, ‘He that winneth souls is wise.’

Neither need we inquire what foundation there is for that opinion of the ancients,
Cf. Origen, Exhortation to Martyrdom (A.D. 235), II.14-16, 30, 47-51; see also J. L. von Mosheim, Institutiones historiae ecclesiasticae (1726; Eng. tr. by J. Murdock, 1841), ‘Century’ I, v.9-15. For a review of the history and theology of martyrdom in the early church, see W. H. C. Frend, Martyrdom and Persecution in the Early Church (1963), pp. 199-200, 353-58. that the same reward awaits those who give witness to the truth by dying for it themselves, or by saving a soul from death. For whether the reward of a martyr and of a winner of souls be one or no, sure we are that either is sufficient;
sure we are that either is eminently glorious above that of common Christians. Neither God’s Word nor his attributes suffer us to doubt that different men will have different rewards in heaven; ‘that as one star differeth from another star in glory, so it will be in the resurrection of the dead’;
Cf. 1 Cor. 15:41-42. ‘every man shall receive his own reward, according to his own labour.’
1 Cor. 3:8 [note the echo here of ‘the gospel of moral rectitude’]. And some of the highest of these rewards are promised to the converters of sinners. All who are admitted into the presence of God will be glorious, but these shall exceed in glory. ‘They that are wise shall shine as the brightness of the firmament, [and] they that turn many to righteousness as the stars for ever and ever!’
Cf. Dan. 12:3.

After inspiring a sinner with this generous resolution, one step more is to be taken, and that is to fix him in it, to guard him from a relapse, lest his last state should be worse than the first;
See Matt. 12:45. in spite of the arts which his old enemies will soon use to draw him from his steadfastness, to ‘hold up his goings in the way, that his footsteps slip not’;
Cf. Ps. 17:5. to watch over his soul till he has fully proved the whole armour of God, that he may be able to ‘wrestle with principalities and powers, with the rulers of the darkness of this world, to quench every fiery dart of the wicked, and, having done all, to stand’.
Cf. Eph. 6:11-13. He who hath thus enlightened the understanding and regulated the will of a sinner, he hath eminently advanced the glory of God, he hath rescued a fellow-creature from destruction, he hath procured an exceeding reward for himself; he hath won a soul; he is wise.
A later hand has added ‘truly’ before ‘wise’.
